# CopyTree Ignore File
#
# Tuned for exporting CandidCue as AI context: ship the code that IS the
# product, drop everything else. What survives is src/, src-tauri/src/, the
# shipped crates (cc-align, cc-stt), the schema and capability files that
# define real runtime behaviour, and the build config that wires them up.
#
# The bar for staying in: does reading this file teach you how the app works?
# Measured data, generated output, prose about the app and tests that restate
# the source all fail that bar, however interesting they are on their own.
#
# Nothing here is lost — bring any subtree back on demand:
#   copytree --scope bench --scope-include-ignored -S   # one subtree
#   echo 'docs/**' >> .copytreeinclude                  # permanently
#
# This file is the "everything real" baseline, and it is at its floor. After
# the last pass ~98% of what survives is .ts/.svelte/.rs source, spread evenly
# (src-tauri/src 20%, src/stage 19%, crates 15%, src/shared 9%, routes 9%,
# components 6%) with no non-code lump left to cut. Every remaining byte is a
# file somebody wrote to make the app behave. If the export still feels too
# big, that is the app being big — do NOT go hunting here for another 20%.
#
# Three reasons no further rule helps, all measured:
#
#   - ~42% of the export is inline Rust `#[cfg(test)]` modules (34% of the
#     Rust; `align/mod.rs` is 82% test tail, `logging.rs` 72%, `ledger.rs`
#     55%) and comment lines (49% of the TS/Svelte). Both sit INSIDE files
#     that have to stay. Ignore rules work per file, so nothing reaches them.
#   - The mass is in a handful of genuinely enormous source files —
#     stores/session.ts 180 KB, ScriptLayer.svelte 164 KB, tracker.rs 156 KB,
#     Settings.svelte 150 KB, StageApp.svelte 118 KB. Real logic, all of it.
#   - `.github/workflows/*` and `.github/SECURITY.md` are FORCE-included by
#     copytree itself. They survive both this file and an explicit `-x`, so
#     don't add a rule for them and wonder why it does nothing (~26 KB).
#
# To narrow, scope per task rather than editing this file:
#   copytree --scope src/stage --scope src/shared -S
#
# If you truly need the default smaller, the only lever left is dropping whole
# working subsystems — see "Opt-in subsystem cuts" at the bottom, which lists
# the candidates with measured sizes, switched off.
#
# Syntax is .gitignore syntax, including directory pruning: once `dir/**` is
# excluded the walker never descends into it, so a `!dir/sub/file` negation
# needs `!dir/sub/` re-included first.
#
# Already handled elsewhere, so absent here: node_modules, dist, target,
# coverage, .git, .vscode/.idea and all binary media (copytree's global
# excludes); .env, .playwright/, *.log and build artifacts (.gitignore).

# ── Tests ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The suite is roughly half the export and mostly restates behaviour the source
# already shows. Four homes: the tests/ tree, Rust integration tests in both
# workspaces, and colocated *.test.ts sitting next to the module they cover.
# (Rust `#[cfg(test)]` modules live inside the source files and necessarily
# come along — no pattern can reach them.)
tests/**
src-tauri/tests/**
crates/*/tests/**
**/*.test.ts
**/*.spec.ts

# ── Benchmarks ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ~4.5 MB, over half the export, and none of it is the app. Three parts:
#
#   bench/runs*/     captured replay corpora — events.jsonl recogniser traces,
#                    gold.json ground truth, manifests, spoken transcripts.
#                    Pure measurement data. Also Git LFS, so without a fetch
#                    these are ~130-byte pointers that read as truncated code.
#   bench/results/   generated scores (already gitignored, listed for the prune)
#   crates/cc-bench/ the offline harness. It ports the shipping TypeScript
#                    follower into Rust to score the rendered stage, which
#                    makes it a SECOND implementation of motion — actively
#                    confusing as context, because it looks like product code
#                    and isn't. src/stage/motion/ is the real one.
#
# bench/README.md and bench/REPORT.md are good engineering prose about what the
# alignment engine measures, but they document results, not the codebase.
bench/**
crates/cc-bench/**

# A one-off measurement probe, not a code path. src-tauri/src/speech/ +
# crates/cc-stt/src/local.rs are what actually runs.
crates/cc-stt/examples/**

# ── Dev-only scaffolding ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# mock-backend.ts is the fake `invokeOrMock` falls back to when the Tauri
# runtime is absent (plain-browser dev). It is stub responses, not behaviour —
# reading it teaches you the shape of the IPC surface, which contracts/ already
# states properly, and the fake's guesses about what commands return.
src/control/services/mock-backend.ts

# Model-fetch tooling. How the 161 MB recogniser bundle lands on disk is a
# build step (`pnpm models:fetch`), not something the app does at runtime.
scripts/**

# ── Specifications ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ~660 KB of design prose describing an earlier architecture that included live
# coaching and an OpenAI sidecar — both since removed. The README says it
# plainly: where the specs and the code disagree, the code is the authority.
# So the specs are actively misleading as context. Read them deliberately, not
# by default.
docs/**

# ── Data blobs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Word lists and vocabularies: large, flat, and they teach nothing the code
# using them doesn't say better.
src-tauri/models/**/tokens.txt

# ── Lockfiles ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Enormous, generated, zero signal. package.json and the Cargo.toml manifests
# carry the dependency story. (copytree would otherwise emit them
# structure-only.) Both cargo workspaces have their own.
pnpm-lock.yaml
src-tauri/Cargo.lock
crates/Cargo.lock

# ── Test and build tooling ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Config for how the project is tested, not what it does. vite.config.ts and
# svelte.config.js stay — they define the multi-window entry points and the
# aliases every import in src/ resolves through.
playwright.config.ts
vitest.config.ts
vitest.dom.config.ts

# ── Binary assets and packaging ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Listener video, posters, fonts and app icons. Mostly binary and already
# dropped by copytree's global excludes; this prunes the walk and the stray
# manifest that rides along.
assets/**
src-tauri/icons/**

# ── Generated schemas ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tauri emits these from the capability files during build. The capabilities
# themselves are the authored artefact and stay in.
src-tauri/gen/**

# ── Legal, licensing and release prose ───────────────────────────────────────
# Text that ships WITH the product but describes none of it. The privacy
# policy is policy language, not a code path. licenses/ holds the verbatim
# upstream licence text the downloadable model ships under (Apache-2.0), and
# THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md is generated wholesale by scripts/generate-notices.mjs,
# already excluded above.
#
# Bring the whole lot back for a licensing or store-submission task:
#   copytree --scope legal --scope-include-ignored -S
legal/**
THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md
LICENSE

# Release notes. A record of what changed and when — useful to a human picking
# up the project, useless to anything reasoning about how the code works, and
# it grows every release.
CHANGELOG.md

# Not reachable from here, so no rule for it: src-tauri/models/x-asr-480ms/
# ATTRIBUTION.md (2.2 KB) is re-included by a negation in that directory's own
# .gitignore, which is evaluated after this file and wins. `copytree explain`
# on it shows the rule here matching and then being overridden — don't add one
# back and trust `ignore check`'s "redundant" verdict to mean it worked.
# catalogue.json beside it STAYS regardless: the download flow reads it.

# ── Duplicated orientation prose ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# AGENTS.md restates CLAUDE.md's structure-and-commands tour in shorter form,
# and CLAUDE.md is the one that is kept current. Two tellings of the same
# layout is worse than one — the reader has to diff them to find out which
# is stale. README.md stays: it is written for a person, not an agent, and it
# carries the honest-gaps section nothing else does.
AGENTS.md

# Git plumbing. The only thing .gitattributes configures is LFS for bench/**,
# which is excluded above, so it documents a subtree the export cannot see.
.gitattributes

# ── Housekeeping ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Empty directory markers with nothing to say.
**/.gitkeep

